# Builder contract — {{TITLE}}

Every builder agent reads this file first and obeys it exactly. It is a
contract, not advice. The orchestrator does not read it into its own context;
agents are given the path.

## What you produce

**Exactly one file**: `{{FRAGMENT_DIR}}/<your slide's filename>.html`. You write
that file and nothing else. Not the storyboard, not `index.html`, not shared
CSS, not another slide, not an asset copy. Another agent is working on the
neighbouring slide in the same batch, and the orchestrator owns everything
shared.

## Structure

- One root `<section>` and nothing outside it. No wrapping element, no sibling.
- `id` on that section equals the filename stem. `s07-market.html` gives
  `<section id="s07-market">`.
- The section carries a ground class (`ground-dark` or `ground-light`) and its
  archetype class (`arch-*`).
- **Layout attaches to a direct child `<div class="wrap">`, never to the
  section.** reveal writes an inline `display: block` on the presented section,
  which outranks every selector including `#id`. A fragment previewed on its
  own still looks right, which is exactly why this bug ships if you ignore it.
  Archetypes that need a wrap: {{WRAP_REQUIRED}}.
- Per-slide CSS goes in one `<style>` block inside the section. **Every
  selector in it contains `#your-section-id`.** An unscoped selector will
  restyle other slides; lint rejects it.
- No `<script>`. Ever. The deck also ships as a PDF and as a PowerPoint export;
  anything that only exists after JS runs does not survive either.
- No nested `<section>`, no reveal fragment classes, no vertical stacks. Flat
  horizontal slides only, so the PDF page count equals the slide count.

## Canvas

Fixed **{{CANVAS_WIDTH}}x{{CANVAS_HEIGHT}}**. Sizes are px on that canvas; reveal scales the
whole thing. Use the tokens in `css/tokens.css` for colour, type, and spacing.
Do not introduce a hex value or a font size that is not a token, and do not
restate a token's value.

Respect the safe area: `var(--margin-slide)` of clearance on all four edges,
except deliberate full-bleed elements (`.bleed`, `.scrim`).

## Numbers and sourcing

- Every number comes from a registry row named in your slide's manifest entry.
  You may not introduce a number that is not in your declared set. If the slide
  needs one that is missing, say so in `openQuestions` and leave it out.
- Put the citation as an **HTML comment inside the element holding the
  number**: `<span class="stat-num">628<!-- F-233 --></span>`. Inside SVG
  `<text>` too. Every naked number needs one.
- **Registry IDs never render.** Comments only.
- Data slides carry a `<footer class="src">` naming public sources and dates:
  "Source: Frost & Sullivan (IPO prospectus, June 2025); company FY2025 annual
  results, March 2026."
- Every number is dated on the face. An undated number is the single most
  common defect in a deck like this. A unit-less number is a liability, not a
  claim.
- One hero number per panel. A delta belongs in that number's caption, never as
  a second numeral competing with it.

## Copy

Follow the deck's voice rules at `{{VOICE_PATH}}`. Read them; do not assume.
The recurring ones:

- Every superlative carries a qualifier and a source, or it is deleted.
- Targets sound like targets: "target", "plan", "signed". Never "will exceed".
- Borrowed equity does the positioning. Named partners and verified rankings
  say more than self-description.
- Non-English terms are glossed on first use, then either form is fine. Wrap
  them: `<span lang="zh-Hans">圣贝拉</span>`, so the CJK face applies and the
  subsetter sees the glyphs.
- No em dashes in slide copy. Use a middot, a colon, or parentheses.
- No absolutes about operations. "Zero tolerance for errors" is liability
  phrasing, not luxury phrasing.

## Charts

- **Invoke the `dataviz` skill before writing the first line of chart code.**
- Hand-authored inline SVG with real `<text>` elements. Never raster text,
  never `<foreignObject>`. Text must stay selectable, translatable, and
  subsettable.
- Static only. No tooltips, no hover states, no animation: the deck ships as a
  PDF.
- House tokens only. One axis, never dual. Thin marks, recessive hairline grid,
  direct labels rather than a legend where the layout allows.
- Rebuild a chart from the registry values. Never screenshot a chart from a
  reference deck.

## Assets

- You do **not** copy files. Reference them as `media/<name>` and list what you
  need in `assetsNeeded` with source and destination. The orchestrator stages
  them after your batch. A missing `media/<name>` at your build time is
  expected.
- **Read the actual image file before you place it.** Catalog descriptions have
  been wrong: swapped captions, a 128px thumbnail described as a photo, a
  co-brand lockup that was not one. Reading the file is what catches this.
- No third-party logo or mark without cleared rights. Render credentials as set
  type instead.

## Reference renders

If you are given a render of a previous version of this slide, it is a
**layout and atmosphere reference only**. It is never a pixel target and it is
**never a source of numbers**. The registry is the only number source.

## When you finish

Fill the structured report: the file you wrote, the section id, the assets you
need staged, the fact IDs you cited, any flags you surfaced on the face, any
deviation from the storyboard block and why, and any open question. A
deviation you do not report is a defect; a deviation you report becomes a
storyboard amendment.
